name measuring-hate-speech-annotations
# The set's file is Parquet; it is written out beside it as a table (extracted/): the first row the columns' own
# names, every value as the file holds it.
# The set's card (README.md): "39,565 comments annotated by 7,912 annotators, for 135,556 combined rows", and
# ("Key dataset columns"):
#   comment_id - unique ID for each comment          annotator_id - unique ID for each annotator
#   text - lightly processed text of a social media post
#   hate_speech_score - continuous hate speech measure
#   sentiment, respect, insult, humiliate, status, dehumanize, violence, genocide, attack_defend, hatespeech -
#     ordinal label that is combined into the continuous score
#   annotator_severity - annotator's estimated survey interpretation bias
# The recipe's reading, not the card's words: a row is one annotator's reading of one comment. What the annotator
# answered of the comment (the ten labels, and whom the comment targets) the annotator says, of the comment. What the
# set measured of the comment, and what it holds of the annotator, the set says. The card's feature list does not say
# whom infitms, outfitms, std_err and hypothesis belong to; it lists them in the order hate_speech_score, text,
# infitms, outfitms, annotator_severity, std_err, annotator_infitms, annotator_outfitms, hypothesis, and the recipe
# records the four of the comment.
# A comment is its text; comment_id is the set's key to it. An annotator stands within the set: [the set, annotator_id, the id].
match train-*.tsv

tier row by \n
header
escaped \
tier field by \t

thing row text
key row comment_id
own annotator_id
content text platform hate_speech_score infitms outfitms std_err hypothesis sentiment respect insult humiliate status dehumanize violence genocide attack_defend hatespeech target_* annotator_*

# The card: "hate_speech_score - continuous hate speech measure"
relation "continuous hate speech measure" hate_speech_score
# Kennedy et al. (2020), the card's paper: "Social media comments were collected from three popular platforms:
# YouTube, Reddit, and Twitter."
relation "Social media comments were collected from" platform
# Kennedy et al. (2020): "We used the identity relevance and hate speech hypothesis scores to create five
# stratification bins"
relation "hate speech hypothesis scores" hypothesis
# Sachdeva et al. (2022), the card's other paper: "an infit mean-squared statistic, a rater fit diagnostic"
relation "infit mean-squared statistic" infitms annotator_infitms
# The card: "annotator_severity - annotator's estimated survey interpretation bias"
relation "annotator's estimated survey interpretation bias" annotator_severity
# The ten survey items, in the prompts Sachdeva et al. (2022) give them ("Table 1: Survey items and ordinal responses
# given to annotators for each comment"):
#   Sentiment "How would you describe the sentiment of this comment?"
#   Respect "Is this comment respectful towards the group(s) you previously identified?"
#   Insult "Do you agree or disagree with the following statement: This comment is insulting towards the group(s) you
#   previously identified."
#   Humiliate "... This comment is humiliating towards the group(s) you previously identified."
#   Status "This comment states that the group(s) you previously identified is:"
#   Dehumanize "... This comment dehumanizes the group(s) you previously identified (e.g., by comparing them to an
#   animal)."
#   Violence "... This comment calls for using violence against the group(s) you previously identified."
#   Genocide "... This comment calls for the deliberate killing of a large group of people from the group(s) you
#   previously identified."
#   Attack-Defend "Is the comment attacking or defending the group(s) you previously identified?"
#   Hate speech "Does this comment contain hate speech, defined as ..."
# (the engine keeps a relation's words to 63 bytes, so each is the prompt's statement up to the group)
relation "How would you describe the sentiment of this comment?" sentiment
relation "Is this comment respectful towards the group(s)" respect
relation "This comment is insulting towards the group(s)" insult
relation "This comment is humiliating towards the group(s)" humiliate
relation "This comment states that the group(s)" status
relation "This comment dehumanizes the group(s)" dehumanize
relation "This comment calls for using violence against the group(s)" violence
relation "calls for the deliberate killing of a large group of people" genocide
relation "Is the comment attacking or defending the group(s)" attack_defend
relation "Does this comment contain hate speech" hatespeech
# Obligations:
#   outfitms, annotator_outfitms, std_err: neither the card nor its two papers word them (Laplace-Engine#32).
#   target_* (target_race_asian, ..., target_race, ...): each column is one identity group or subgroup of the
#   checklist the annotator ticked, true where ticked; the answer is the group the column stands for, which the
#   recipe cannot yet read as the value (Laplace-Engine#32).
#   annotator_* (gender, trans, educ, income, ideology, age, and their ticked subgroups): what the corpus reports of
#   the annotator, worded nowhere; with the people the corpus names (Laplace-Engine#29).
attest row platform hate_speech_score infitms outfitms std_err hypothesis
attest row sentiment respect insult humiliate status dehumanize violence genocide attack_defend hatespeech target_* by annotator_id
# The annotator columns are named one by one: annotator_id, the annotator itself, is not said of itself.
attest row annotator_severity annotator_infitms annotator_outfitms annotator_gender annotator_trans annotator_educ annotator_income annotator_ideology annotator_age annotator_gender_* annotator_transgender* annotator_cisgender annotator_education_* annotator_income_* annotator_ideology_* annotator_race_* annotator_religion_* annotator_sexuality_* of annotator_id
